/* book: lead: A pass transistor uses a MOSFET as a switch: its gate is the control and data flows between its source and drain. An nMOS passes a clean 0 but stops a 1 one threshold below its gate; a pMOS passes a clean 1 but stops a 0 one threshold above ground. A transmission gate (TG) puts an nMOS and a pMOS in parallel with complementary controls, so together they pass both rails. Pass-transistor logic build */
