/* book Worked example: Q: An nMOS pass transistor has its gate and input at VDD = 1.8 V. Vt0 = 0.5 V, γ = 0.4 V1/2 and 2φF = 0.7 V. Find the highest output level without and with the body effect. What does a transmission gate pass?
   1. Without body effect: VY = 1.8 − 0.5 = 1.30 V.
   2. With body effect VY = 1.8 − Vtn(VY). Try VY = 1.30 V: Vtn = 0.5 + 0.4(√2.0 − √0.7) = 0.5 + 0.4(1.414 − 0.837) = 0.731 V, so VY = 1.069 V.
   3. Try VY = 1.069 V: Vtn = 0.5 + 0.4(√1.769 − 0.837) = 0.5 + 0.4(1.330 − 0.837) = 0.697 V, so VY = 1.103 V.
   4. Try VY = 1.103 V: Vtn = 0.5 + 0.4(√1.803 − 0.837) = 0.702 V, so VY = 1.098 V. The values have settled: VY ≈ 1.10 V with Vtn ≈ 0.70 V.
   5. Transmission gate: the pMOS (gate at 0 V, source at 1.8 V) stays on until Y reaches 1.8 V, so Y = 1.8 V.
   Answer: An nMOS alone passes 1.30 V ignoring body effect and only about 1.10 V with it (Vtn rises to 0.70 V); the transmission gate passes the full 1.8 V. */
