/* book Worked example 2: Q: The weak 1.10 V level drives a CMOS inverter with VDD = 1.8 V, kn = kp = 200 µA/V² and Vtn = |Vtp| = 0.5 V. Is the pMOS off? Estimate the static current.
   1. pMOS VSG = 1.8 − 1.10 = 0.70 V > |Vtp| = 0.5 V, so the pMOS is still on, with overdrive 0.20 V.
   2. The nMOS has VGS = 1.10 V (overdrive 0.60 V) and pulls the output low, so the pMOS is saturated and limits the current.
   3. I ≈ (200/2)(0.20)² = 100 × 0.04 = 4 µA, flowing all the time the weak 1 is held.
   Answer: The pMOS stays on and about 4 µA flows from VDD to ground, which is why weak levels are restored or a TG is used. */
